<body>
<div id="progress" role="presentation"></div>

<header class="bar">
  <div class="bar-in">
    <div class="brand">Why Is the Sky Blue? <span>· 天空为什么是蓝色的？</span></div>
    <nav aria-label="Sections">
      <a href="#s1">1 · Toddler / 幼儿</a>
      <a href="#s2">2 · High school / 高中生</a>
      <a href="#s3">3 · Physics PhD / 物理学博士</a>
    </nav>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Language view">
        <button type="button" data-v="split" aria-pressed="true">Split</button>
        <button type="button" data-v="en" aria-pressed="false">EN</button>
        <button type="button" data-v="zh" aria-pressed="false">中文</button>
      </div>
      <button type="button" class="icon-btn" id="theme" aria-label="Toggle colour theme" title="Toggle theme (T)">◐</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<main>
  <div class="intro">
    <h1><span class="en">Why Is the Sky Blue?</span><span class="zh">天空为什么是蓝色的？</span></h1>
    <p>Three explanations, from simplest to most rigorous — English alongside 简体中文, section by section.</p>
    <p lang="zh-Hans">三种解释，从最简单到最严谨——英文与简体中文逐节并排对照。</p>
    <div class="legend">
      <b style="--c:var(--l1)"><i class="dot"></i>Level 1 · concrete, no jargon</b>
      <b style="--c:var(--l2)"><i class="dot"></i>Level 2 · Rayleigh law + eye response</b>
      <b style="--c:var(--l3)"><i class="dot"></i>Level 3 · polarizability, optical depth, colorimetry</b>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ 1 ============ -->
  <section class="pair" id="s1" style="--c:var(--l1)">
    <div class="head">
      <span class="lvl">1</span>
      <h2><span class="en">For a 3-Year-Old</span><span class="zh">讲给 3 岁小朋友</span></h2>
    </div>

    <article class="pane en" lang="en">
      <span class="tag">EN</span>
      <p class="lede">The sky is blue because sunlight plays a game called “scatter.”</p>
      <p>Sunlight looks white, but it’s actually made of lots of colors — like a rainbow hiding inside it!</p>
      <p>When sunlight comes down to Earth, it bumps into tiny little air bits all over the sky. When it bumps, the colors go flying in all directions.</p>
      <p>The blue color is the wiggly, jiggly one that gets knocked around the <em>most</em>. So blue gets thrown everywhere, up, down, and all around — and that’s the color that reaches your eyes when you look up.</p>
      <p class="callout"><strong>Try this:</strong> Look up during the day. Blue everywhere! Now look at the sun near bedtime (don’t stare right at it!). The light has to travel through <em>lots</em> more air to get to you, and by then almost all the blue has been knocked away, so you see the leftover reds and oranges. That’s a sunset. 🌅</p>
    </article>

    <article class="pane zh" lang="zh-Hans">
      <span class="tag">中</span>
      <p class="lede">天空是蓝色的，因为阳光在玩一个叫“散开”的游戏。</p>
      <p>阳光看起来是白色的，可它其实是由好多好多颜色凑成的——就像彩虹偷偷藏在里面啦！</p>
      <p>阳光落到地球上的时候，会撞上天空中到处都是的、小小的空气颗粒。一撞，颜色就朝四面八方飞出去。</p>
      <p>蓝色是最爱动、最爱晃的那一个，所以被撞得<em>最</em>厉害。于是蓝色被甩得到处都是，上上下下、四面八方——你抬起头的时候，钻进你眼睛里的就是它。</p>
      <p class="callout"><strong>试试看：</strong>白天抬起头看天，到处都是蓝色！再在快睡觉的时候看看太阳（千万不要直视它！）。这时候的光要穿过<em>好多好多</em>空气才能到你眼睛里，等它到的时候，蓝色几乎全被撞跑了，剩下的红颜色和橙颜色你就看见了。这就是日落呀。🌅</p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 2 ============ -->
  <section class="pair" id="s2" style="--c:var(--l2)">
    <div class="head">
      <span class="lvl">2</span>
      <h2><span class="en">For a High School Student</span><span class="zh">讲给高中生</span></h2>
    </div>

    <article class="pane en" lang="en">
      <span class="tag">EN</span>
      <p class="lede">Short version: Rayleigh scattering + the design of your eyes.</p>

      <h3>Sunlight is a mixture</h3>
      <p>Sunlight is roughly a 5800 K blackbody spectrum — a continuous spread of wavelengths from ultraviolet through visible (~400–700 nm) into infrared. We perceive it as “white” because it covers the whole visible band fairly evenly.</p>

      <h3>The atmosphere scatters short wavelengths far more strongly</h3>
      <p>Air is mostly N₂ and O₂. Individual molecules are ~0.3 nm across — much smaller than visible wavelengths — so they act as tiny induced dipoles driven by the oscillating electric field of incoming light. This is <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>, and its cross-section goes as:</p>
      <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Rayleigh dependence</span><span class="m">σ</span>(<span class="m">λ</span>) ∝ <span class="frac"><span class="n">1</span><span class="d"><span class="m">λ</span><sup>4</sup></span></span></div>
      <p>So blue light (~450 nm) scatters about <strong>(650/450)⁴ ≈ 4.3×</strong> more strongly than red light (~650 nm). Over the long path through the atmosphere, blue photons get redirected out of the direct solar beam and into your line of sight from every direction. Look anywhere <em>away</em> from the sun and you catch this redirected blue — that’s the sky’s glow.</p>

      <h3>Why not violet? (The classic gotcha)</h3>
      <p>Violet (~400 nm) scatters even more strongly than blue by the <span class="m">λ</span><sup>−4</sup> law. Three things stop the sky from looking violet:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>The Sun emits <strong>less</strong> violet than blue.</li>
        <li>Our <strong>S-cones</strong> are less sensitive at 400 nm, and violet stimulates the S, M, and L cones in a mix we read as “blue-ish.”</li>
        <li>The scattered light is a <strong>broad mixture</strong> of violet + blue + some green, which our visual system perceives as cyan-blue.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Sunsets, clouds, and the rest of the family</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Sunsets:</strong> At low sun angles, the path length through the atmosphere is ~10× longer (roughly ∝ 1/sin of the elevation angle). Multiple scattering strips out nearly all blue and green from the direct beam, leaving reds and oranges.</li>
        <li><strong>Clouds / fog / milk:</strong> Water droplets are ~1000× larger than the wavelength, so scattering becomes <strong>wavelength-independent (Mie scattering)</strong>. All colors scatter equally → white.</li>
        <li><strong>Why space is black:</strong> No atmosphere, nothing to scatter light sideways into your eyes.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="callout"><strong>The one-sentence answer:</strong> The atmosphere is a wavelength-dependent mirror that leaks blue light out of the sunbeam in every direction, and our eyes are built to notice it.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="pane zh" lang="zh-Hans">
      <span class="tag">中</span>
      <p class="lede">简版：瑞利散射 + 你眼睛的构造方式。</p>

      <h3>阳光是一种混合物</h3>
      <p>阳光大致接近 5800 K 的黑体辐射谱——从紫外、经过可见光（约 400–700 nm）、一直延伸到红外的连续波长分布。我们之所以觉得它是“白色”，是因为它相当均匀地铺满了整个可见光波段。</p>

      <h3>大气对短波长的散射强得多</h3>
      <p>空气主要是 N₂ 和 O₂。单个分子的直径只有约 0.3 nm，远小于可见光波长，所以它们就像一个个被入射光振荡电场驱动的微小感应偶极子。这就是<strong>瑞利散射（Rayleigh scattering）</strong>，其散射截面满足：</p>
      <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">瑞利依赖关系</span><span class="m">σ</span>(<span class="m">λ</span>) ∝ <span class="frac"><span class="n">1</span><span class="d"><span class="m">λ</span><sup>4</sup></span></span></div>
      <p>因此蓝光（约 450 nm）的散射强度约为红光（约 650 nm）的 <strong>(650/450)⁴ ≈ 4.3 倍</strong>。在穿越大气层的漫长路径上，蓝色光子从太阳直射光束中被“折”出来，从各个方向进入你的视线。只要望向<em>偏离</em>太阳的任意一片天空，你就会捕捉到这些被改道的蓝光——那就是天空的辉光。</p>

      <h3>为什么不是紫色？（经典陷阱）</h3>
      <p>按 <span class="m">λ</span><sup>−4</sup> 定律，紫光（约 400 nm）比蓝光散射得更强。有三件事让天空看起来不是紫色：</p>
      <ol>
        <li>太阳辐射中紫光的成分本来就比蓝光<strong>少</strong>。</li>
        <li>我们的 <strong>S 视锥细胞</strong>在 400 nm 处灵敏度较低，而且紫光刺激 S、M、L 三种视锥所产生的组合，被我们读作“偏蓝”。</li>
        <li>散射光其实是紫光 + 蓝光 + 部分绿光的<strong>宽谱混合</strong>，我们的视觉系统把它感知为青蓝色。</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>日落、云，以及这一家子的其他成员</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>日落：</strong>太阳高度角很低时，光穿过大气的路径长约 10 倍（大致 ∝ 1/sin 高度角）。多次散射几乎把直射光束中的蓝光和绿光全部剥离，只剩下红与橙。</li>
        <li><strong>云 / 雾 / 牛奶：</strong>水滴比波长大出约 1000 倍，于是散射变得<strong>与波长无关（米氏散射，Mie scattering）</strong>。所有颜色被等量散射 → 白色。</li>
        <li><strong>为什么太空是黑的：</strong>没有大气，就没有东西把光侧向散射进你的眼睛。</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="callout"><strong>一句话答案：</strong>大气是一面随波长变化的镜子，它把蓝光从太阳光束里向四面八方漏出来，而我们的眼睛正好被设计成能察觉它。</p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 3 ============ -->
  <section class="pair" id="s3" style="--c:var(--l3)">
    <div class="head">
      <span class="lvl">3</span>
      <h2><span class="en">For a Physics PhD</span><span class="zh">讲给物理学博士</span></h2>
    </div>

    <article class="pane en" lang="en">
      <span class="tag">EN</span>
      <p class="lede">Short version: the sky is blue because the linear susceptibility of air is a real, non-resonant function of <span class="m">ω</span> below the first electronic resonance, and the relevant optical depth for scattering is ≪ 1 while the optical depth for direct transmission is ≫ 1 at 450 nm.</p>

      <h3>Scattering cross-section from first principles</h3>
      <p>Treat N₂/O₂ as bound-electron oscillators driven far below their resonance frequencies (first allowed electronic absorption of N₂ is the Lyman–Birge–Hopfield / B–X band near 120 nm; O₂’s Schumann–Runge onset ~175 nm). In the non-resonant dipole limit, the molecular polarizability is essentially frequency-independent in the visible except for the dispersion correction, and the single-molecule Rayleigh cross-section is</p>
      <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Single-molecule Rayleigh cross-section</span>
        <span class="m">σ</span> =
        <span class="frac"><span class="n">24<span class="m">π</span><sup>3</sup></span><span class="d"><span class="m">λ</span><sup>4</sup><span class="m">N</span><sup>2</sup></span></span>
        <span class="paren">(</span><span class="frac"><span class="n"><span class="m">n</span><sup>2</sup> − 1</span><span class="d"><span class="m">n</span><sup>2</sup> + 2</span></span><span class="paren">)</span><sup>2</sup>
        <span class="m">F</span><sub>DK</sub>
      </div>
      <p>with <span class="m">F</span><sub>DK</sub> ≈ 1.03–1.14 the King depolarization ratio accounting for the anisotropy of the molecular polarizability tensor (pure isotropic Rayleigh would be 6π/3 rather than 3π/2 geometry — the factor of ~4 discrepancy is exactly this anisotropy term). Numerically, at STP, <span class="m">σ</span><sub>N₂</sub>(550 nm) ≈ 5.1×10<sup>−31</sup> m², giving a volume scattering coefficient <span class="m">β</span> ≈ 1.4×10<sup>−5</sup> m<sup>−1</sup> and a scattering mean free path of ~70 km at 550 nm.</p>
      <p>Two important refinements over the textbook <span class="m">λ</span><sup>−4</sup>:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Aerosols / Mie regime:</strong> The real atmosphere contains an aerosol extinction term that is much weaker in its spectral dependence (Ångström exponent <span class="m">α</span> ≈ 0.5–1.5 rather than 4). Under hazy conditions the sky desaturates toward white in the horizon direction — this is the dominant correction to the “pure <span class="m">λ</span><sup>−4</sup>” story and is why the sky near the horizon is paler than at zenith.</li>
        <li><strong>Multiple scattering &amp; ozone:</strong> In the blue/violet the atmosphere is not single-scattering; the source function includes a multiple-scattering integral, and at Chappuis-band wavelengths (≈450–650 nm) ozone absorption adds a weak, spectrally structured attenuation. Rigorous radiance requires solving the transfer equation with the Rayleigh (Henyey–Greenstein-like, actually the dipole phase function <span class="m">p</span>(<span class="m">Θ</span>) = ¾(1 + cos<sup>2</sup><span class="m">Θ</span>) plus depolarization terms) and Mie phase functions.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The color is a measurement outcome, not a property of the light</h3>
      <p>This is the part usually skipped. The spectral radiance of the clear daytime sky peaks in the violet/blue (roughly 440–460 nm at zenith, shifting redder toward the horizon). It does <strong>not</strong> look violet because color is a projection of the spectrum onto three cone fundamentals. The CIE-converted chromaticity of zenith sky radiance lands near (<span class="m">x</span>, <span class="m">y</span>) ≈ (0.17, 0.20) — in the blue region of the diagram, not the violet line. Metameric “sky blue” is produced by the <em>combination</em> of the physical spectrum and the cone response matrix. Change the observer (a tetrachromatic bird with a UV-sensitive cone sees a genuinely different — and for many species, UV-patterning-rich — sky) and you change the answer. This is a real, measurable effect: avian sky polarization/UV vision is documented in navigation studies.</p>

      <h3>Counterfactuals that confirm the mechanism</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Sunset red (long-path extinction):</strong> <span class="m">τ</span>(<span class="m">λ</span>) = <span class="m">β</span>(<span class="m">λ</span>)·<span class="m">m</span>, with air mass <span class="m">m</span> ≈ 1/sin <span class="m">h</span>. Direct-beam transmittance <span class="m">e</span><sup>−τ</sup> at <span class="m">h</span> ≈ 2° gives <span class="m">τ</span><sub>blue</sub> ≫ 1, so the residual direct beam is red. The same physics that makes the sky blue makes sunsets red — the two observations are a single consistency check on <span class="m">λ</span><sup>−4</sup>.</li>
        <li><strong>Altitude:</strong> Blue intensity falls with altitude as <span class="m">N</span> decreases (<span class="m">β</span> ∝ <span class="m">N</span>); above most of the atmosphere (rocket/ISS photos) the sky is black and the sunlit limb is a thin blue arc — a direct map of the exponential density profile <span class="m">ρ</span>(<span class="m">z</span>) = <span class="m">ρ</span><sub>0</sub><span class="m">e</span><sup>−z/H</sup>, <span class="m">H</span> ≈ 8 km. The <em>depth</em> of the blue band (~100 km visible scale) is a measurement of the scale height.</li>
        <li><strong>Polarization:</strong> Single Rayleigh scattering is maximally polarized (100% for ideal isotropic scatterers, ~80% with depolarization) at a 90° scattering angle — verifiable with a rotating polarizer pointed 90° from the sun. Mie scattering at small phase angles is much less polarized, so polarization measurements cleanly separate molecular from aerosol contributions (this is precisely how AERONET / Sentinel-3 retrieve aerosol optical depth).</li>
        <li><strong>Exoplanet analog:</strong> Rayleigh scattering atmospheres produce a characteristic rising spectral slope in transit transmission spectra (∝ d ln <span class="m">R</span><sub>p</sub> / d ln <span class="m">λ</span> ≈ −<span class="m">α</span>). Detected on e.g. HAT-P-11b. The mechanism is universal, not Earth-specific.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The honest, compressed answer</h3>
      <p>Below the first electronic resonance, air’s polarizability is essentially real and constant, so induced-dipole radiation gives <span class="m">σ</span> ∝ <span class="m">ω</span><sup>4</sup>. Because column optical depth is O(0.1–0.5) at 550 nm and O(1–3) at 400 nm along typical sight lines, the sky is a <em>differential</em> filter whose escape probability rises steeply with frequency; the observed chromaticity is then the cone-primary projection of that spectrum. “Blue” is the intersection of a <span class="m">λ</span><sup>−4</sup> cross-section, a 5800 K Planck function, an exponential density profile, ozone’s Chappuis band, an aerosol continuum, and a three-cone visual system.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="pane zh" lang="zh-Hans">
      <span class="tag">中</span>
      <p class="lede">简版：天空之所以蓝，是因为在第一条电子共振之下，空气的线性电极化率是关于 <span class="m">ω</span> 的实函数、非共振函数；而且在 450 nm 处，与散射相关的光学深度 ≪ 1，而直射透射的光学深度 ≫ 1。</p>

      <h3>从第一性原理推散射截面</h3>
      <p>把 N₂/O₂ 看作被驱动在远低于其共振频率处的束缚电子振子（N₂ 第一条允许的电子吸收带是 120 nm 附近的 Lyman–Birge–Hopfield / B–X 带；O₂ 的 Schumann–Runge 带起始于约 175 nm）。在非共振偶极极限下，分子极化率在可见光区基本上与频率无关（除了色散修正），单分子瑞利散射截面为</p>
      <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">单分子瑞利散射截面</span>
        <span class="m">σ</span> =
        <span class="frac"><span class="n">24<span class="m">π</span><sup>3</sup></span><span class="d"><span class="m">λ</span><sup>4</sup><span class="m">N</span><sup>2</sup></span></span>
        <span class="paren">(</span><span class="frac"><span class="n"><span class="m">n</span><sup>2</sup> − 1</span><span class="d"><span class="m">n</span><sup>2</sup> + 2</span></span><span class="paren">)</span><sup>2</sup>
        <span class="m">F</span><sub>DK</sub>
      </div>
      <p>其中 <span class="m">F</span><sub>DK</sub> ≈ 1.03–1.14 是 King 去偏振比（depolarization ratio），用于修正分子极化率张量的各向异性（若为纯各向同性瑞利散射，几何因子应为 6π/3 而非 3π/2 —— 这个约 4 倍的差异恰好就来自各向异性项）。数值上，在标准温压下 <span class="m">σ</span><sub>N₂</sub>(550 nm) ≈ 5.1×10<sup>−31</sup> m²，给出体散射系数 <span class="m">β</span> ≈ 1.4×10<sup>−5</sup> m<sup>−1</sup>，在 550 nm 处的散射平均自由程约 70 km。</p>
      <p>相对教科书式 <span class="m">λ</span><sup>−4</sup> 的两个重要修正：</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>气溶胶 / 米氏散射区：</strong>真实大气含有气溶胶消光项，其光谱依赖弱得多（Ångström 指数 <span class="m">α</span> ≈ 0.5–1.5，而不是 4）。霾天时，靠近地平线方向的天空会向白色“去饱和”—— 这是对“纯 <span class="m">λ</span><sup>−4</sup>”图像最主要的修正，也是为什么地平线附近的天空比天顶更淡。</li>
        <li><strong>多次散射与臭氧：</strong>在蓝/紫波段大气并不处于单次散射状态；源函数中包含多次散射积分，而在 Chappuis 吸收带波长（约 450–650 nm）臭氧吸收会叠加一个弱但具有光谱结构的衰减。严格求解辐射亮度需要求解辐射传输方程，其中瑞利相函数（类似 Henyey–Greenstein，实际是偶极相函数 <span class="m">p</span>(<span class="m">Θ</span>) = ¾(1 + cos<sup>2</sup><span class="m">Θ</span>)，再叠加去偏振项）与米氏相函数都要考虑。</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>颜色是一个测量结果，而不是光的属性</h3>
      <p>这是通常被跳过的一环。晴天白昼天空的光谱辐射亮度峰值在紫/蓝区（天顶约 440–460 nm，朝地平线方向红移）。它看起来<strong>并不</strong>是紫色，因为颜色是光谱在三种视锥基本函数上的投影。天顶天空辐射亮度经 CIE 换算后的色度落在 (<span class="m">x</span>, <span class="m">y</span>) ≈ (0.17, 0.20) 附近 —— 位于色度图的蓝色区域，而非紫线之上。“天空蓝”这一同色异谱色，是由物理光谱与视锥响应矩阵<em>共同</em>产生的。换掉观测者（拥有紫外敏感视锥的四色视觉鸟类，看到的是一个真正不同的、对许多物种而言富含紫外图案的天空），答案就随之改变。这是真实可测的效应：鸟类对天空偏振/紫外的视觉在其导航研究中已有文献记录。</p>

      <h3>可以验证该机制的反事实情形</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>日落红（长路径消光）：</strong><span class="m">τ</span>(<span class="m">λ</span>) = <span class="m">β</span>(<span class="m">λ</span>)·<span class="m">m</span>，其中气团 <span class="m">m</span> ≈ 1/sin <span class="m">h</span>。在 <span class="m">h</span> ≈ 2° 时直射透射率 <span class="m">e</span><sup>−τ</sup> 对应 <span class="m">τ</span><sub>blue</sub> ≫ 1，因此残余直射光束呈红色。让天空变蓝与让日落变红的是同一套物理——这两个观测构成对 <span class="m">λ</span><sup>−4</sup> 的一次一致性检验。</li>
        <li><strong>高度：</strong>随着 <span class="m">N</span> 减小，蓝色强度随高度下降（<span class="m">β</span> ∝ <span class="m">N</span>）；在大气绝大部分之上（火箭/国际空间站照片）天空是黑的，而被阳光照亮的边缘是一道薄薄蓝色弧——这正是指数密度分布 <span class="m">ρ</span>(<span class="m">z</span>) = <span class="m">ρ</span><sub>0</sub><span class="m">e</span><sup>−z/H</sup>（<span class="m">H</span> ≈ 8 km）的直接映射。蓝色“层”的<em>厚度</em>（可见尺度约 100 km）本身就是对标高的一次测量。</li>
        <li><strong>偏振：</strong>单次瑞利散射在 90° 散射角处偏振度最大（理想各向同性散射体为 100%，计入去偏振约 80%）——把偏振片指向与太阳成 90° 的方向并旋转即可验证。米氏散射在小相角处的偏振度低得多，因此偏振测量可以干净地把分子贡献与气溶胶贡献分开（AERONET / Sentinel-3 反演气溶胶光学厚度用的正是这一原理）。</li>
        <li><strong>系外行星类比：</strong>具有瑞利散射的大气在凌星透射光谱中会产生特征性的上升光谱斜率（∝ d ln <span class="m">R</span><sub>p</sub> / d ln <span class="m">λ</span> ≈ −<span class="m">α</span>）。已在例如 HAT-P-11b 上探测到。该机制是普适的，并非地球特有。</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>诚实而压缩的答案</h3>
      <p>在第一条电子共振之下，空气的极化率基本上是实且近似恒定的，因此感应偶极辐射给出 <span class="m">σ</span> ∝ <span class="m">ω</span><sup>4</sup>。由于在典型视线上，550 nm 的柱光学深度为 O(0.1–0.5)、400 nm 为 O(1–3)，天空是一个<em>差分</em>滤波器，其逃逸概率随频率急剧上升；而观测到的色度则是该光谱在视锥三色原色上的投影。所谓“蓝色”，是 <span class="m">λ</span><sup>−4</sup> 截面、5800 K 普朗克函数、指数密度分布、臭氧 Chappuis 带、气溶胶连续消光背景与三视锥视觉系统这六者的交汇点。</p>
    </article>
